# AI Agent Organization

This directory defines the responsibilities, authority, and collaboration model for AI agents working on the NODS platform.

The objective is to ensure that every AI agent operates within a clearly defined role, producing consistent, production-quality software while preserving the architecture of the platform.

---

# Philosophy

AI agents are specialized engineers.

Each agent has a defined area of responsibility.

No single agent is responsible for every aspect of the system.

This separation of responsibilities improves consistency, reduces architectural drift, and encourages better engineering decisions.

---

# Chain of Responsibility

```
User
│
▼
Principal Software Architect
│
├──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐
▼              ▼              ▼              ▼
Backend     Frontend      Database      DevOps
│              │              │              │
└──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘
               │
               ▼
          Code Reviewer
               │
               ▼
         Quality Assurance
```

The Principal Software Architect defines the direction.

Implementation agents execute the design.

The Reviewer validates the implementation.

The QA agent validates correctness.

---

# Agent Hierarchy

## Level 1

Principal Software Architect

Authority:

- System architecture
- Domain boundaries
- Package organization
- Design decisions
- Technical strategy

This role has the highest architectural authority.

---

## Level 2

Specialist Engineers

Includes:

- Backend
- Frontend
- Database
- DevOps

Each specialist is responsible only for their assigned domain.

Specialists must follow the Architect's decisions.

---

## Level 3

Reviewer

The Reviewer does not create features.

The Reviewer evaluates:

- Architecture
- Maintainability
- Readability
- SOLID
- DDD compliance
- Coding standards
- Security concerns

---

## Level 4

Quality Assurance

QA validates:

- Correctness
- Edge cases
- Tests
- User scenarios
- Regression risks

QA focuses on software quality rather than implementation.

---

# Communication Rules

Every AI agent must:

- Stay within its responsibilities.
- Avoid making decisions outside its authority.
- Escalate architectural conflicts to the Architect.
- Explain assumptions explicitly.
- Never invent undocumented requirements.

---

# Escalation Rules

When uncertainty exists:

Implementation Agent
→ Architect

When implementation is complete:

Implementation Agent
→ Reviewer

When review is complete:

Reviewer
→ QA

Only after QA approval should implementation be considered complete.

---

# Collaboration Principles

Agents collaborate.

Agents do not compete.

Different opinions are encouraged when supported by technical reasoning.

Architecture always has priority over implementation convenience.

---

# Decision Authority

| Decision | Responsible Agent |
|------------|-------------------|
| Architecture | Architect |
| Domain Model | Architect |
| API Design | Backend |
| Database Schema | Database |
| UI/UX | Frontend |
| Infrastructure | DevOps |
| Code Review | Reviewer |
| Testing Strategy | QA |

If multiple agents disagree, the Architect has the final recommendation.

---

# Shared Responsibilities

Every agent is expected to:

- Follow the approved architecture.
- Follow coding standards.
- Respect dependency rules.
- Produce production-quality work.
- Write or support testing.
- Prefer simplicity over unnecessary complexity.

---

# Success Criteria

The AI organization is successful when:

- All generated code feels like it was written by one engineering team.
- Architectural integrity is preserved.
- Business logic remains consistent.
- Technical debt is minimized.
- Development remains predictable as the platform grows.

Every agent contributes to the same long-term objective: building a maintainable, scalable, production-ready SaaS platform.